1
00:00:00,651 --> 00:00:02,125
This is Proof Of Forest.

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:05,195
Carbon credits that exist only if the forest does.

3
00:00:05,895 --> 00:00:08,190
Voluntary carbon markets run on trust.

4
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:15,438
Investigations have found forest credits sold for growth that never happened, and the same tonne counted more than once.

5
00:00:15,438 --> 00:00:22,807
Verification means expensive site audits that small projects can't afford, and buyers can't check the evidence themselves.

6
00:00:22,807 --> 00:00:29,511
So we built a system where the evidence is public, the check is automatic, and every tonne has a serial number.

7
00:00:30,228 --> 00:00:31,997
It works in three layers.

8
00:00:31,997 --> 00:00:33,058
First, measure.

9
00:00:33,058 --> 00:00:44,096
Our engine pulls free Copernicus Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, masks out clouds, and compares the vegetation inside a project with a control ring around it.

10
00:00:44,096 --> 00:00:45,157
Second, attest.

11
00:00:45,157 --> 00:00:53,930
Three independent oracles each re-run the whole analysis from the raw satellite data, and sign only if every number matches.

12
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:54,921
Third, settle.

13
00:00:54,921 --> 00:01:05,251
An E-R-C 1155 smart contract mints credits only when two of the three oracles agree, and the numbers also pass the contract's own on-chain policy.

14
00:01:05,953 --> 00:01:08,804
Here is the registry, with three case studies.

15
00:01:08,804 --> 00:01:11,222
All of them run on real satellite data.

16
00:01:11,924 --> 00:01:14,850
First, Smritivan in Bhuj, Gujarat.

17
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:18,894
A Miyawaki forest planted on a barren hillside.

18
00:01:18,894 --> 00:01:20,271
Drag the slider.

19
00:01:20,271 --> 00:01:22,508
In 2019 the slope is bare.

20
00:01:22,508 --> 00:01:24,745
By 2025, it's dense green.

21
00:01:25,446 --> 00:01:30,373
In the N-D-V-I greenness index, the project went from 0.14 to 0.43.

22
00:01:30,373 --> 00:01:37,802
But the surrounding city also greened a little, so we only credit the difference: a net gain of 0.22.

23
00:01:37,802 --> 00:01:44,347
It holds across all thirty-six scene pairs, with one hundred percent cloud-free coverage.

24
00:01:45,049 --> 00:01:47,882
That's almost fifteen hectares of new canopy.

25
00:01:47,882 --> 00:01:52,729
After an uncertainty deduction, five hundred and forty one tonnes of C O two.

26
00:01:53,432 --> 00:01:59,123
But could a developer just draw the boundary around whatever happened to turn green?

27
00:01:59,123 --> 00:02:01,969
So the engine tries to prove itself wrong.

28
00:02:01,969 --> 00:02:08,541
It drops the same polygon at two hundred random spots nearby, and analyses each one the same way.

29
00:02:08,541 --> 00:02:10,845
The best random patch gained 0.10.

30
00:02:10,845 --> 00:02:14,165
Smritivan gained 0.22, more than all two hundred.

31
00:02:14,165 --> 00:02:22,499
The result also holds if the boundary moves by twenty metres, and all of the new canopy stays green through the dry season.

32
00:02:22,499 --> 00:02:24,125
That's trees, not grass.

33
00:02:24,828 --> 00:02:27,282
Anyone can relay the signed attestation.

34
00:02:27,282 --> 00:02:30,410
I'll submit it, and the contract mints the credits.

35
00:02:30,410 --> 00:02:37,649
One batch, with unique serial numbers, and twenty percent held back in a buffer pool, in case the forest is ever lost.

36
00:02:38,349 --> 00:02:39,187
Now, a fake.

37
00:02:39,187 --> 00:02:47,572
This hypothetical developer claims three hundred and sixty five hectares of reforestation in Hasdeo Arand, Chhattisgarh.

38
00:02:47,572 --> 00:02:49,878
The satellite shows the opposite.

39
00:02:49,878 --> 00:02:53,581
Dense forest in 2019, an open-cast coal mine by 2025.

40
00:02:53,581 --> 00:02:56,586
The greenness index fell from 0.56 to 0.09.

41
00:02:56,586 --> 00:03:02,315
When the signed verdict is submitted, the contract records the rejection on-chain.

42
00:03:02,315 --> 00:03:05,250
No credits, and a permanent public record.

43
00:03:05,952 --> 00:03:08,096
The third case is the subtle one.

44
00:03:08,096 --> 00:03:10,760
A claim on the Banni grasslands in Kutch.

45
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:14,789
Inside the polygon, the greenness index jumped by almost half.

46
00:03:14,789 --> 00:03:18,688
A simple before and after check would happily issue credits.

47
00:03:19,392 --> 00:03:23,262
But zoom out to the landscape, and everything turned green.

48
00:03:23,262 --> 00:03:26,869
2018 was a drought year, and 2020 had a strong monsoon.

49
00:03:26,869 --> 00:03:31,198
The control ring rose by the same amount, so the net gain is zero.

50
00:03:31,198 --> 00:03:37,823
It's also no better than random land nearby, and most of the new green fades again in the dry season.

51
00:03:37,823 --> 00:03:39,463
That's grass, not forest.

52
00:03:39,463 --> 00:03:42,283
Greenwashing caught, and rejected on-chain.

53
00:03:42,984 --> 00:03:44,507
Back to the real credits.

54
00:03:44,507 --> 00:03:48,893
The developer sells two hundred and fifty tonnes to a buyer, Acme Steel.

55
00:03:49,594 --> 00:03:56,474
Now acting as the buyer, they retire one hundred and twenty tonnes against their 2026 Scope 1 emissions.

56
00:03:56,474 --> 00:04:03,355
Retiring burns the tokens and assigns serial numbers one to one hundred and twenty to Acme, permanently.

57
00:04:03,355 --> 00:04:07,258
Here is the certificate, tied to the satellite report hash.

58
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:09,788
Anyone can verify a serial.

59
00:04:09,788 --> 00:04:13,377
Serial forty two: retired, in certificate number one.

60
00:04:13,377 --> 00:04:15,950
It can never be sold or claimed again.

61
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:20,419
And the ledger shows every verification, including the rejections.

62
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:32,748
Proof Of Forest uses only free, public data, verifies a project in about ninety seconds, and is backed by fifty two automated tests, including the real satellite reports, end to end.

63
00:04:32,748 --> 00:04:40,862
Sentinel-2 revisits every forest on Earth every five days, so projects can be monitored continuously, not once every few years.

64
00:04:40,862 --> 00:04:45,206
Every number in this demo is reproducible from the open source repo.

65
00:04:45,206 --> 00:04:45,845
Thank you.
